WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.640
How does a growing branch know which thin layer to stop in?

2
00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.170
A gradient can bring an axon near its target.

3
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.170
But a thin layer is either the right one or not.

4
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:16.693
Choices like that often call for discrete labels.

5
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:22.697
In the retina, the inner plexiform layer is a thin band where three kinds of cell

6
00:00:22.697 --> 00:00:23.697
make synapses.

7
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:26.977
It is divided into thinner sublaminae.

8
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:30.737
Each cell type sends its processes into one or a few.

9
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.470
In chick retina, two related proteins are Sidekick-1 and Sidekick-2.

10
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.060
Largely separate groups of neurons make them.

11
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.340
Each group's processes end in different sublaminae.

12
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:48.323
Each protein binds its own kind, which is called homophilic binding.

13
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.567
Sidekick-1 sticks to Sidekick-1.

14
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.450
Does the label only mark the band, or help choose it?

15
00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.530
Predict. Make neurons produce a Sidekick they normally lack.

16
00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.750
Where should their processes end?

17
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:13.513
Their processes shifted toward the sublamina where that Sidekick was already

18
00:01:13.513 --> 00:01:14.513
concentrated.

19
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:17.063
Adding the label shifted the arbor.

20
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:25.813
Later, Yamagata and Sanes added Dscam and DscamL, making four labels.

21
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:28.743
These molecules bias a choice.

22
00:01:29.543 --> 00:01:31.223
They are one part of how layers form.

23
00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:37.776
In mouse retina, cells lacking DSCAM or DSCAML1 still reach the right sublaminae.

24
00:01:38.766 --> 00:01:41.186
Here is the idea to carry through this lesson.

25
00:01:41.186 --> 00:01:45.426
A match between identical proteins says only, we are the same.

26
00:01:46.366 --> 00:01:48.146
The cell decides what same means.

27
00:01:49.116 --> 00:01:53.216
When a group of cells shares a sticky label, their processes are drawn together.

28
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:55.600
Here, same means stick.

29
00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.470
Takeaway: in chick retina, shared sticky labels help like processes settle in

30
00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.890
matching sublaminae, alongside other cues.
